 "poem": "Though, in a little walk by river,\nThe lovers' love was sharpest to behold;\nBut 'twere not their rosy lips they behold\nMore clearly than the last happy old.\nThey look'd at each other for awhile,\nTill the hour of death came, and parted.\nWell! weep not over these tears that arise,\nNor let them stain thy mind's precious pages;\nFor time will make them bright enough to\nA certain day seemeth still enough.\nThe world is gone, and she who grieves anew\nMust live with this brief past for evermore. The",
